uh and i'm joined by the shadow  environment secretary jim mcmahon good  morning mr mcmahon  [Music]  um is labour going to support the  government's uh plans on  the how to deal with this latest wave of  omicron including uh the pre-travel  tests and so forth  well of course it was the labour party  who were calling for uh pre-testing to  take place because we're very concerned  that the government consistently threw  out the pandemic have been very late in  making the calls that are required to  keep our borders safe very late in terms  of trying to spread the control uh the  control the spread of that virus and  what we want to do is to make sure that  we don't jeopardize  out the worst thing in the world after  all the sacrifices that we've made is  that a new variant comes in and  completely takes the rug from under that  program and so it's very important the  government get a grip it's very  important that we take swift action and  frankly it shouldn't be for the  opposition to be continually one step  ahead of the government the government  needs to take control themselves  yeah but um getting a grip  would that involve or should that have  involved uh  bringing in plans to work from home or  social distancing  or indeed a ban on christmas parties um  whether or not you're ahead of the  government is that what you wanted  we've been very clear that common sense  measures that taken together keep us  safer is the way to to go and so of  course if a workplace is unsafe then  action will need to be taken but many  workplaces can be safe providing that  appropriate action is taken to maintain  social distancing to make sure that  people are supported to self-isolate and  to take sick leave if they need to and  that includes decent pay to allow people  to do that we've been very clear that  common sense interventions are far  better than a blunt instrument  in this and we want to make sure that  after the sacrifice that we've made and  listen most of us uh last year at  christmas were adhering to the rules and  we made sacrifices we didn't see our  loved ones we didn't mix we didn't have  parties in the office we adhered to the  rules after all that sacrifice that  we've made we want to look forward to  this christmas and the only way to do  that is they're going to have a very  clear plan for them to kind of get on  with the booster vaccination  roll out and to make sure that  throughout the course of that that the  messaging is really clear because the  worst thing in the world is that the  government say one thing but then do  another and people look at the actions  of the government and they get a  confused message  i'm not entirely clear about your your  own message here  would a clear plan  involve saying to people you should not  have christmas parties for example  well we've been very clear to the  government to bring forward the plan  based on the scientific evidence and  advice and it's got to be a coherent  plan we've been very clear that we do  not believe that christmas passes should  be cancelled we believe that hospitality  has been for a very difficult period and  it's very important for hospitality that  we continue uh in the way that we want  to but that can only happen if we're not  undermined by new strain variants go  into the country that completely take  apart the vaccination rollout  so what about vaccine passport for big  events would you  would you have insisted on that  we've always wanted to take a common  sense and evidence-based approach and of  course it wasn't that long ago that we  were saying very clearly that for large  gatherings uh that testing should be  taking place to make sure that if people  were carrying covered they weren't able  to spread it across a large group of  people but we've also been very clear  that it's got to be a balanced approach  and so you know you wouldn't necessarily  need it for the football for the  restaurants uh we don't want to have  unnecessary rules in place that really  undermine uh confidence in hospitality  confidence in going out over christmas  but equally we do want people to be  cautious you know we are not on the  other side of this uh yet there's still  many cases that are being identified  there's still many people who are in  hospital uh through covert and we don't  know yet the extent of the new variant  so your cautious common sense approach  is what's needed  well i don't spend too much time on this  but what you've told me is you will back  the steps of the government's taken you  would not do more than they uh have said  that they want to do on christmas  parties you would not introduce vaccine  passport you're basically telling me  exactly the same as a government  minister would tell me which is to say  let's be cautious let's be common  central and so on what's your problem  with what the government's done then  well first of all it was labor that  called for the pre-departure testing to  take place and the government will later  come in to that conclusion uh themselves  it's the labour party who's calling for  five hundred thousand test  booster vaccinations to be administered  every day and at the moment the  government's only reaching 370 000 and  so we are very clear about what's needed  we need to make sure that people are  resilient and have the booster  vaccination but the government needs to  do far more to make it accessible they  need to be invested in pop-up facilities  whether that's in  campuses on the high street in  workplaces to make sure that it is  accessible and people take that boost of  vaccination by doing that of course  they're more resilient to the virus but  equally you know the vaccination program  as it stands could easily be undermined  by a new variant that completely  undermines it and so that's why it's  really important that we have strong  borders but in doing so make sure that  we understand the implications of that  for aviation for tourism  of course we will begin to see  consumer confidence diminish it's very  important the government have a single  plan to make sure the common sense  interventions on one hand fully see  through the implications of that to make  sure that our economy and our societies  are supported  um  we've been in the north this this week  uh mr mcmahon and um  you of course you're an oldham mp you  you'll have seen the  impact of storm arwen um  do you think that what's been done is  adequate and  frankly do you think as  some people said to me uh in the  northeast if that that a  storm had hit the southeast uh do you  think that the response would have been  the same as it has been  well i'm in alden today but i was  further off uh earlier in the week when  i went to county durham to meet  residents who are directly affected by  the power outage you know and that was  now six days into the storm when i uh  visited uh one of the first things i did  in my new role in the shadow cabinet  and when you hear the real lived  experience of people you know i beggar's  belief  that in a national emergency it wasn't  uh considered a national priority you  know where was boris johnson why wasn't  cobra called to deal with this why is it  taking a week for the army to be called  in you know and i firmly believe had  this have been the case where 20 000  properties were without power in surrey  or in london the government would  absolutely have treated it with more  seriousness and i think for people  encountered who i spoke to they felt  isolated they felt angry and they felt  forgotten  michael gove who is the leveling up  secretary has been talking this week we  understand about introducing  greater devolution to different parts of  england including for example the idea  of  what i think he calls governors which  are sort of like state governors in the  united  states you were a local authority leader  would you um favor that sort of  devolution  i do think we have a very fragmented  system of devolution in england uh in a  way that is confused it's not as clear  as scotland uh wales and northern  ireland and the truth is the creation of  the metro mass has given sub-regions a  voice but the counties are voiceless  and actually if you look at the way the  government are directing money aside  from the red wall seats that they want  to support quite a lot of that is being  sent through uh mayoral combined  authorities at the disadvantage of many  of our counties and so the government do  need a single plan to make sure that  governance accountability and investment  in england is where it needs to be  whether governors are the right approach  i'm not sure i think you know kirsten  has been really clear that when we talk  about the power imbalance in this  country is because people in the  communities where they live don't  believe they've got power over the  direction of the places where they live  and so before we start talking about new  politicians being created we ought to  talk about whether people feel able to  drive the direction of the places where  they live in the communities where they  live and i think for many people they  don't feel that way particularly by the  way because the government continually  take power away from local people i mean  just look at one of the biggest things  that actually affects your area is  physical development building work  construction well actually the  government have taken that power away  from many communities in favor of  developers who quite often are not  working in the interests of the local  community and so if we're talking about  where power says it should be absolutely  rooted in the communities where people  live  okay well let's talk about power let's  talk about how you get your hands on on  some um you came second on in a  by-election in bexley this week which is  encouraging you had a 10 swing from the  conservatives which is pretty good um  but according to john curtis who is the  great authority on these things even a  10 swing repeated all over the country  still wouldn't give you a majority there  is a massive mountain to climb for labor  isn't there so we understand fully the  challenge uh ahead of us uh but also we  reflect on the progress that we have  made you know in 2019 the election was a  disaster you know people weren't willing  to even consider labour being in  government but now case dahmer is our  leader as a right to be heard but now  what we need to do is to convince people  that we've got a plan an alternative  plan for this country and that's very  much the kind of framing of the new  shadow cabinet that's very much the  framing that kirst dharma is keen to get  out there and i think actually now the  restrictions have eased and we're able  to get around the country more we are  able to speak to people in the places  where they live in a direct way that i  do believe is cutting through  and actually you know the bexar  by-election for the tourists was a  disaster uh not least of all it would  have put you know if that was uh seen  through in a general election it would  have put labour very close to majority  government but the truth is that every  day that passes the conservatives are  getting worse they are being found out  people see them for what they are which  is it's one rule for them and it's  another for everybody else and people  are fed up  with having that limiter  at your first shadow cabinet meeting did  you actually talk about the issue of  your pathway back to power which  for most observers only lies through two  pathways  one  that nigel farage takes enough votes off  the tories  to lose them many of the constituencies  that they now hold  or  that you do a deal with nicola sturgeon  did you discuss at the style cabinet how  it feels to be beholden either to nigel  farage or nicolas sturgeon or both to  get into number ten  well not really because the  conversations that we have at shadow  cabin are not defeatist conversation  they are positive con conversations  they're ambitious uh conversations and  we are ambitious not for ourselves  people who live here and so we're  absolutely clear that with our path to  government with a plan that's been set  out that by the time we get to the  general election  that we will be in a position to form a  majority government that's a project  that's exactly what we're in politics to  do  jim mcmahon thank you very much for your  time this morning

